# versions.env --- the pinned toolchain, in ONE place.
#
# Read by scripts/setup.sh, scripts/setup.ps1 and .github/workflows/*.yml, so a CI
# build and a dev machine stand up the SAME toolchain. Plain KEY=VALUE (no quoting,
# no expansion) precisely so sh, PowerShell and YAML can all parse it trivially.
#
# Bump deliberately: these three decide whether two machines produce the same binary.

# SBCL. Upstream publishes binaries for Linux x86-64 (tarball) and Windows (MSI, x86-64 and
# arm64) only -- there are NO macOS binaries and NO arm64 Linux binaries (none in any of
# 2.6.0 to 2.6.9, checked 2026-10-15, #9). On arm64 Linux, setup.sh asks you to install an
# SBCL yourself and then uses it, with a warning that its version differs from this pin.
#
# Two honest caveats, both verified on CI rather than assumed:
#   macOS follows HOMEBREW, not this pin. There is no upstream macOS binary to pin to, so
#   the machine gets whatever brew carries (2.6.5 when this was written -- one patch
#   behind). --check passes on the mismatch deliberately; it checks presence, not equality.
#   Linux inherits a GLIBC FLOOR from this pin. The 2.6.9 Linux binary needs GLIBC_2.38, so
#   it will not run on ubuntu-22.04 (2.35) -- which is why CI builds on 24.04, and why
#   binaries we produce require glibc 2.39+. Lowering that means building SBCL from source.
SBCL_VERSION=2.6.9

# The SHA-256 of what scripts/setup.sh downloads, VERIFIED before the file is unpacked or
# loaded (#211). On a mismatch setup.sh deletes the file, prints both values and stops.
#
# SBCL_SHA256_X86_64_LINUX is the sbcl-$SBCL_VERSION-x86-64-linux-binary.tar.bz2 that setup.sh
# downloads, so it changes whenever SBCL_VERSION does. Cross-checked against SBCL's own
# signed checksum file, sbcl-$SBCL_VERSION-crhodes.asc on the same SourceForge release: that
# file lists the SHA-256 of the UNCOMPRESSED .tar, so decompress the download and compare
#   bunzip2 -c sbcl-2.6.9-x86-64-linux-binary.tar.bz2 | sha256sum     # c6265653...2cd4, as listed
#   gpg --decrypt sbcl-2.6.9-crhodes.asc                                # Good signature, Christophe Rhodes, key D6839CA0A67F74D9DFB70922EBD595A9100D63CD
# There is no arm64 Linux tarball to pin (#9). macOS installs SBCL from Homebrew, which
# verifies its own packages, so setup.sh downloads nothing there for SBCL.
SBCL_SHA256_X86_64_LINUX=39fac28407405841003e4c2abfbe0d96abd93b7701e8b61cdd35e713943b46d6

# QUICKLISP_LISP_SHA256 is https://beta.quicklisp.org/quicklisp.lisp, the installer setup.sh
# loads on every Unix. That URL is not versioned: if Quicklisp publishes a new one, setup
# stops here until the pin is updated, which is the intended outcome. Update it only after
#   gpg --verify quicklisp.lisp.asc quicklisp.lisp    # Good signature, Quicklisp Release Signing Key, key 307965AB028B5FF7
QUICKLISP_LISP_SHA256=4a7a5c2aebe0716417047854267397e24a44d0cce096127411e9ce9ccfeb2c17

# SBCL_SHA256_X86_64_WINDOWS and SBCL_SHA256_ARM64_WINDOWS are the
# sbcl-$SBCL_VERSION-<arch>-windows-binary.msi files that scripts/setup.ps1 downloads, verified
# before msiexec runs (#211). They change whenever SBCL_VERSION does.
#
# THE CROSS-CHECK IS WEAKER THAN THE LINUX ONE, and this says how. SBCL's signed checksum
# file, sbcl-$SBCL_VERSION-crhodes.asc (good signature, Christophe Rhodes, key
# D6839CA0A67F74D9DFB70922EBD595A9100D63CD), lists only the documentation, the source and the
# x86-64 Linux tarball; it does not cover either MSI, and the MSIs carry no Authenticode
# signature (Get-AuthenticodeSignature: NotSigned). SBCL's GitHub release has no assets. What
# was checked instead, on 2026-10-15 for 2.6.9:
#   - SourceForge's file index (https://sourceforge.net/projects/sbcl/rss?path=/sbcl/2.6.9)
#     gives an MD5 and a size for each MSI, and both match the pinned file:
#       x86-64  md5 2e470bd26d1c1108c1896c9ab4ae615f, 14757888 bytes
#       arm64   md5 1e9ededd6947cb00debc43745c100639, 12861440 bytes
#   - Choosing a second SourceForge mirror from a script was not possible (the mirror hosts
#     need a token SourceForge only issues through its download page), so every download
#     here came through the default mirror. The CI Windows leg downloads the x86-64 MSI
#     independently from GitHub's network, and setup.ps1 refuses it there unless it matches.
# So these pins say "this is the file SourceForge served and indexed on that date". They stop
# a later substitution; they cannot vouch for the first download the way a signature would.
SBCL_SHA256_X86_64_WINDOWS=2be695e44a69ff29f01cf7980f67472cb383e75e9292d5fcd27cf5418b871226
SBCL_SHA256_ARM64_WINDOWS=8d8b394dd9c7922d8bcd43949dd022679f536b225146428d29313a7a593edb20

# The Quicklisp dist snapshot -- what actually pins every external library version.
QUICKLISP_DIST=2026-01-01

# appimagetool -- packages a Linux bundle into a single-file .AppImage (#72). Fetched by
# scripts/build-appimage.sh and VERIFIED against this checksum, on the same doctrine as
# libuv.pin: recorded trust-on-first-use, so a later substitution is loud rather than
# silent. A TAGGED release, never the `continuous` build -- a moving packaging tool is the
# reproducibility hazard the pins exist to remove.
#
# It is the Linux counterpart to NSIS on Windows (installed by CI) and create-dmg on macOS:
# a PACKAGING tool, not a build tool, so it does not breach "sbcl --script is the only
# build driver" any more than NSIS does.
APPIMAGETOOL_VERSION=1.9.1
APPIMAGETOOL_SHA256=ed4ce84f0d9caff66f50bcca6ff6f35aae54ce8135408b3fa33abfc3cb384eb0

# Coalton's commit is NOT pinned here -- it lives in the repo-root `coalton.pin`, which is
# the single source of truth for it (see docs/coalton-upstream.md). Two files naming a
# Coalton commit is the exact reproducibility hazard the pin was created to remove, so
# setup.{sh,ps1} read `coalton.pin` directly.

# SQLite -- `cl-sqlite', under mnemosyne's DEFAULT backend (pre-publication issue 229). Windows is the only
# supported OS that does not supply it under a name the loader looks for: Linux installs
# libsqlite3 from a package (setup.sh, pre-publication issue 198) and macOS ships it, while Windows ships
# `winsqlite3.dll' -- a DIFFERENT NAME that cl-sqlite never tries. It works perfectly if
# you rename it (measured: mnemosyne's whole suite, 360 checks, 36 against the sqlite
# backend), and it is invisible to the loader as configured, which is why the machine that
# reported the sqlite backend green was being carried by an unrelated Delphi install.
#
# Pinned AND checksummed on the same doctrine as appimagetool: a native binary this script
# places on a developer's machine is a supply-chain surface, so a substitution is loud
# rather than silent. Upstream encodes the version in the download name as MMmmppXX --
# 3.54.1 is 3540100 -- which scripts/setup.ps1 derives rather than duplicating here.
#
# The version is the same as sqlite.pin's, which desktop bundles carry (#899); upstream.sexp
# checks that the two agree. sqlite.org publishes SHA3-256: the two zips hashed for the
# SHA-256 lines below have SHA3-256 c2d09c8babb9399277055c057c97a53f1ffd7c5f11d0c1f2118ffdfa39f2daa4
# (x64) and 58a563f64ba4c5df34c3ce667175678e2c1df503b264cd600200eb37aa334201 (arm64), the
# values on sqlite.org's download page (checked 2026-10-24, #765).
SQLITE_VERSION=3.54.1
SQLITE_YEAR=2026
SQLITE_SHA256_X64=f436f53ad8f965af43f3817b42c99c0689e6787f6b7ace208fe38dab7ffc4971
SQLITE_SHA256_ARM64=3a73fa9659ed4d5e0d49757d83134a9c8f54b6915d4a689cbfe59d4db509231c

# WebView2 SDK -- the headers hyperion/hyperion-view/build.ps1 compiles the Windows launcher
# against, fetched from NuGet (#175). bootstrap.lisp runs that script on every Windows machine
# with a C++ toolchain, so this is a download placed on developer machines automatically, and
# it is pinned AND checksummed on the same grounds as appimagetool and SQLite above. The
# checksum is of the .nupkg (9,258,495 bytes for 1.0.4078.44). It was cross-checked when
# recorded: the SHA-512 of the same file equals the packageHash NuGet's catalog publishes for
# this version. Moving the version means updating the checksum in the same commit.
WEBVIEW2_SDK_VERSION=1.0.4078.44
WEBVIEW2_SDK_SHA256=dc4d1d9168df26b830398303e50210b6e1729f6ce5a7ac69d2c766852f489962
